    {
      "id": "chapter-21",
      "number": 21,
      "title": "Chapter 21 — Metrics and review dashboard",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Do not judge a new business only by followers, likes, or total messages sent. Track the stages that connect effort to customer value.\nMetric\tWhat it tells you\nRelevant prospects identified\tWhether you are targeting a real market\nWarm outreach sent\tWhether you are activating existing relationships\nPositive replies\tWhether the message is relevant\nDiscovery conversations\tWhether prospects are willing to discuss the problem\nQualified prospects\tWhether the need, budget, and fit are real\nProposals sent\tWhether you are presenting a clear offer\nCustomers won\tWhether the offer converts\nDelivery completion\tWhether you fulfil what you sell\nCustomer outcome\tWhether the service creates value\nReferrals\tWhether customers are willing to recommend you\nRepeat purchase or continuation\tWhether the service remains valuable\nCash collected\tWhether revenue is turning into cash",
        "21.1 Diagnose the bottleneck\n- Few replies: improve prospect selection and personalisation.\n- Replies but no conversations: improve the question and next step.\n- Conversations but no sales: improve qualification, offer clarity, proof, and pricing.\n- Sales but poor outcomes: improve delivery and customer fit.\n- Good outcomes but few referrals: ask at the right moment and make the introduction easy; also investigate whether customers are comfortable recommending the service.\n- Initial sales but no repeat business: build a useful continuation offer and review ongoing value.\n- Revenue but little cash: review payment terms, collection, costs, and cash management."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "chapter-22",
      "number": 22,
      "title": "Chapter 22 — Final checklist and principles",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Before launching\n- [ ] I have chosen a service I can competently deliver.\n- [ ] I understand the customer and the problem.\n- [ ] I have a clear pilot scope.\n- [ ] I know what evidence I want to collect.\n- [ ] I have contacted relevant warm prospects.\n- [ ] I am transparent about free, paid, and pilot terms.\nBefore charging\n- [ ] I have delivered useful work.\n- [ ] I can show relevant proof.\n- [ ] I have a clear offer.\n- [ ] I understand my delivery costs.\n- [ ] I have a reasonable price.\n- [ ] I can explain the next step.\n- [ ] I can fulfil what I promise.\nBefore trying to scale\n- [ ] Customers are getting value.\n- [ ] Delivery is repeatable.\n- [ ] I have a way to acquire prospects consistently.\n- [ ] I have a referral process.\n- [ ] I have a continuation offer where appropriate.\n- [ ] I know which tasks can be systemised.\n- [ ] I track cash, costs, and customer retention.\nThe core principles\n1. Use what you have. Start with existing skills, interests, experience, and relationships.\n2. Choose a low-cost learning vehicle. A service business can help you learn sales and delivery without requiring a large initial build.\n3. Get proof before scaling. Results and relevant evidence make your offer more credible.\n4. Start with warm contacts. People who know your work may be willing to give you the first opportunity.\n5. Ask for referrals after delivering value. Make it easy for satisfied customers to introduce others.\n6. Use free work strategically. If you do not charge money, be clear about the learning, feedback, or evidence you hope to receive.\n7. Turn proof into a paid offer. Do not stay free because you are afraid to charge.\n8. Create attention through useful content and thoughtful participation. Do not spam communities or treat engagement as consent to be sold to.\n9. Build systems from repeated work. Solve a problem manually, understand it, then create reusable tools and templates.\n10. Niche down when evidence supports it. Focus can improve relevance and delivery efficiency.\n11. Create the next customer step. Do not make every customer journey end after the first purchase.\n12. Do not abandon a working model for status. Evaluate the business by its economics and customer value.\n13. Be consistent. Sustainable inputs create more predictable opportunities.\n14. Learn from friction. Repeated problems reveal where your skill and systems need to improve.\n15. Keep the business honest. Use real proof, clear terms, and promises you can fulfil.\nThe central message is not that one exact sequence guarantees a first $10,000. It is that a beginner can reduce the complexity of starting a business by beginning with a useful service, proving that it works for real customers, and building the sales and delivery systems one step at a time.",
        "The first customer is not the finish line. The real business begins when you can repeatedly find the right people, help them get a meaningful result, collect fair payment, and improve the service so the next customer receives an even better experience."
      ]
    }
